NAME
    core - load or swap the running ROM, or start a console with nothing in it

SYNOPSIS
    core <corename> <path>
    core <corename> --console

DESCRIPTION
    Validates <corename> against the known core registry and <path> against
    that core's supported file extensions, then loads it - reloading
    VenusCore in place if a ROM is already running (a fresh SnesDebugTarget/
    DianaShInterpreter/FrameRecorder get rebuilt afterward; watches/
    breakpoints/cheats registered against the OLD ROM don't survive a swap,
    matching EmuSen.Mistress's own already-accepted 'shell-level state
    resets on reload' convention). Refuses to swap while a frame recording
    is in progress - stop it first (F6). Only registered where a session
    actually exists to reload - EmuSen.Hotaru's own pre-window launch shell
    has a separate, simpler 'core' handler of its own for resolving the
    FIRST ROM (see EmuSen_Frontend_Driver.md §3), since there's nothing to
    reload yet at that point.

WHERE THE ROM MAY BE
    <path> is read as every path in this shell is: relative to the
    current directory, or with a leading '/' from this shell's root, and
    refused if it lands above the root (see 'man hier'). There is one
    exception, because a ROM library usually lives outside the root: an
    absolute path inside the library configured as RomDirectory in
    Preferences (appsettings.json) is taken as it is. The library is only
    ever read, and only by 'core'; no other command reaches it. The host
    is handed the resolved path, so it does not depend on the shell's
    current directory. Hotaru's launch prompt makes the same checks, and
    a ROM named on its command line is the launcher's choice, not a path
    typed here, so it is not checked. Before 2026-10-10 <path> was
    checked only for existence, so any file of the machine could be
    named.

THE CONSOLE WITH NOTHING IN IT
    'core <corename> --console' starts the console <corename> names with
    no game, every drive empty, where an engine of this build says in its
    core info that the console starts empty (core ABI 1.11's
    starts_empty): the PlayStation, whose kernel then shows its screen
    with no disc and its memory card manager. A console no engine starts
    empty is refused with that said. At a launch prompt the console's
    window opens; in a running session the core starts the console in
    place when it is the engine that runs it, and otherwise says to start
    it from the launch prompt. A disc goes in afterwards through the
    frontend's Insert Disc. See Diamond_Shell.md section 1.5.

EXAMPLES
    core playstation --console
    core venus game2.smc
    core snes /Games/other-game.sfc
    core snes /home/player/Roms/SNES/other-game.sfc   (inside RomDirectory)
